// test-error
// test-adr: 016
// ADR-016 / Issue #1306: nested scopes are not allowed. Not "not in v1" -- never.
// C99 5.2.4.1 guarantees only 31 significant initial characters in an external
// identifier, and MISRA C:2012 Rule 5.1 is scored inside that budget: at depth 4
// Scope1__Scope2__Scope3__Scope4__weight is 38 characters and stops being distinct.
// The prohibition is therefore permanent, not a simplification to be revisited.
//
// This fixture is the weld between two expressions of one decision. The rule lives
// in the grammar (scopeMember admits no scopeDeclaration) AND in the recognizer
// that turns the resulting parse failure into E0430. Widen the grammar and the file
// would parse, no error would be produced, and this fixture fails because
// .expected.error exists and nothing matched it. That is what stops the two halves
// from drifting apart -- neither can be changed alone without reddening this file.
//
// NEGATIVE CONTROL: `Legal` below holds every one of the six member kinds
// scopeMember admits -- variable, enum, struct, bitmap, register and function --
// and five of them open a brace. All must stay silent. Without it, a recognizer
// that rejected any brace inside a scope, or any declaration nested in one, would
// pass this fixture while being wrong. Only the nested `scope` is named in
// .expected.error. Verified to transpile clean on its own: exit 0, 8 symbols.

scope Legal {
    u8 plainValue <- 10;

    public enum Mode {
        OFF,
        ON
    }

    public struct Point {
        u32 x;
    }

    public bitmap8 Flags {
        Alpha,
        Rest[7]
    }

    register CTRL @ 0x40000000 {
        DR: u32 rw @ 0x00,
    }

    public u8 read() {
        return this.plainValue;
    }
}

scope OuterScope {
    u8 outerValue <- 10;

    // THE ASSERTION: this is the only construct this fixture rejects.
    scope InnerScope {
        u8 innerValue <- 5;
    }
}

void main() {
    // Empty main for valid C output structure
}
